    {
      "id": "a1_u02_l02",
      "level": "A1",
      "unit": 2,
      "title": "J'ai 25 ans",
      "description": "Say your age with the verb avoir, and meet the numbers from zero to ten.",
      "estimatedMinutes": 13,
      "sections": [
        {
          "type": "learn",
          "title": "Learn",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Saying your age",
              "body": "In French you don't say \"I am 25 years old\" — you say \"I HAVE 25 years\": \"J'ai vingt-cinq ans.\" The verb is \"avoir\" (to have). To ask someone's age, say \"Quel âge as-tu ?\" (informal) or \"Quel âge avez-vous ?\" (formal).",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "J'ai vingt-cinq ans.", "english": "I am 25 years old." },
                { "french": "Quel âge as-tu ?", "english": "How old are you? (informal)" },
                { "french": "Quel âge avez-vous ?", "english": "How old are you? (formal)" },
                { "french": "Elle a neuf ans.", "english": "She is nine years old." },
                { "french": "Moi, j'ai sept ans.", "english": "Me, I am seven years old." }
              ],
              "tip": "Never use \"être\" for age — \"Je suis 25 ans\" is a classic mistake. Always \"J'ai 25 ans\"."
            },
            {
              "heading": "Numbers 0–10",
              "body": "You need numbers to say your age. Here are zero to ten: zéro (0), un (1), deux (2), trois (3), quatre (4), cinq (5), six (6), sept (7), huit (8), neuf (9), dix (10). We'll practise bigger numbers in the Numbers unit.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "un, deux, trois", "english": "one, two, three" },
                { "french": "quatre, cinq, six, sept", "english": "four, five, six, seven" },
                { "french": "huit, neuf, dix", "english": "eight, nine, ten" },
                { "french": "Deux et trois font cinq.", "english": "Two and three make five." },
                { "french": "Quatre et six font dix.", "english": "Four and six make ten." }
              ],
              "tip": "The p in \"sept\" is silent: it sounds like \"set\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
