        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Elision: j'habite, d'où",
              "body": "French drops the vowel of small words like \"je\" and \"de\" before a vowel sound — this is called elision. Since the h of \"habiter\" is silent, you write \"j'habite\" (never \"je habite\"). Likewise \"de\" + \"où\" gives \"d'où\".",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "j'habite", "english": "I live", "note": "je + habite" },
                { "french": "d'où", "english": "from where", "note": "de + où" },
                { "french": "j'ai", "english": "I have", "note": "je + ai" },
                { "french": "l'adresse", "english": "the address", "note": "la + adresse" },
                { "french": "j'aime", "english": "I like", "note": "je + aime" }
              ],
              "tip": "The apostrophe replaces exactly one dropped vowel — never leave a space around it."
            }
          ]
        },
